{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789263972,"nonce":"YpZoc16eOgOZ1IVyChdjO/3xHMY=","message":"90ac91c: value speculation with a real deoptimisation exit. Baseline code of a promotable body records the kind of every call result and every argument (one byte per register in BytecodeFunction::result_kinds); the top tier inserts GuardNumAt after each used call whose result was only ever a Num and GuardNum at entry for such arguments; TypeSpecialize unboxes the arithmetic behind them and drops guards on values it already knows are Num. GuardNumAt carries the bytecode offset past the call and the registers live there (DeoptReg: a value, or a range rebuilt from its bounds so converted range loops keep no allocation); wren_deopt_at resumes the method in the interpreter through VM::resume_method_sync and returns its result to the native caller. Only bound methods (engine.method_binding) are speculated on; a site is skipped when a live register is not a value the compile clone still defines. Promotion now counts only the baseline's own ticks, emitted at every loop header, so the profile exists before the top tier compiles.\n\nNUC (x86_64, LLVM 21): bench/ash/nbody 3.48 s -\u003e 2.16 s (off 7.2 s; Ash AOT 0.72 s); bench/ash/binary_trees 6.3 s -\u003e 1.23 s (off 6.6 s); mandelbrot 0.47 s unchanged; method_call/closure_call 0.11/0.10 unchanged; delta_blue 0.14 -\u003e 0.15, bench/method_call 0.06 (short programs still pay LLVM compiles, ~40-100 ms per body).\n\nWhat remains in nbody's inner loop per getter: the hit-flag branch, fields-pointer and field loads, and the guard's tag check (about 8 instructions where Ash's typed bytecode has one load); on x86 the deopt live state adds register pressure (the 22-value snapshots in mandelbrot's pixel loop cost 25% until the range objects were materialised instead of kept). Next candidates: class-level field type profiles so inlined getters and direct field reads are typed without a per-load check; fewer deopt points by guarding once per receiver; a cheaper LLVM pipeline for short programs.","files":null}]}